---
// Security, data handling and the full permission reference (docs/14 §5.6). Every statement here
// is checked against the template (through the generated data) or the code; open decisions say
// so rather than promise (data location for production, retention, contacts: docs/14 §11).
import Faq from '../components/Faq.astro'
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro'
import PermissionTable from '../components/PermissionTable.astro'
import { START_URL, TEMPLATE_URL, billingRole, claims, facts, permissions, resourceRole, sessionHours, setupRole } from '../config'
import { SECURITY_FAQ } from '../data/faq'
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'

const title = 'Security and permissions | Cloudoptics'
const description =
  'How Cloudoptics reads your AWS account: read-only roles, every IAM permission in plain language, where data is kept and how to remove access.'
const hours = sessionHours === 1 ? 'one hour' : `${sessionHours} hours`
const h2 = 'text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight'
const sectionCls = 'mt-14'
const sections = [
  { id: 'how-access-works', label: 'How access works' },
  { id: 'permissions', label: 'Every permission' },
  { id: 'data', label: 'Where data is kept' },
  { id: 'sign-in', label: 'Sign-in and MFA' },
  { id: 'our-practice', label: 'How we build Cloudoptics' },
  { id: 'reporting', label: 'Reporting a vulnerability' },
  { id: 'template', label: 'Download the template' },
]
---

<Base title={title} description={description} faq={SECURITY_FAQ}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 pt-12 sm:px-6 lg:pt-16">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-link">Security</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-balance">Read-only access, written down action by action</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-lg text-secondary">
      This page is for the person who signs off access to your AWS account. Every permission below is generated from the
      template you deploy (version {facts.templateVersion}), and our build fails if the two disagree.
    </p>

    <nav aria-labelledby="on-this-page" class="mt-8 rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-1 p-5">
      <h2 id="on-this-page" class="text-sm font-semibold">On this page</h2>
      <ol class="mt-3 grid gap-x-6 gap-y-1 text-sm sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
        {sections.map((s) => <li><a class="inline-flex min-h-6 items-center text-link underline" href={`#${s.id}`}>{s.label}</a></li>)}
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <section id="how-access-works" aria-labelledby="how-access-works-title" class={sectionCls}>
      <h2 id="how-access-works-title" class={h2}>How access works</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3">
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <Icon name="check" class="mt-1 h-5 w-5 text-good-ink" />
          <span>
            You create one CloudFormation stack, <code>{claims.stackName}</code>, in {facts.region}. It creates two IAM roles
            in your account: <code>{billingRole.roleName}</code> and <code>{resourceRole.roleName}</code>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <Icon name="check" class="mt-1 h-5 w-5 text-good-ink" />
          <span>
            Cloudoptics assumes those roles with AWS STS. Each role trusts only the Cloudoptics principal, and only when the
            request carries the ExternalId that is unique to your workspace.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <Icon name="check" class="mt-1 h-5 w-5 text-good-ink" />
          <span>Each session lasts at most {hours}. You never give us access keys, and we hold none for your account.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <Icon name="check" class="mt-1 h-5 w-5 text-good-ink" />
          <span>
            Both roles allow only read actions (Get, Describe, List, View). The build of this site checks every action in
            the template for that, and fails if a write action appears in either role.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <Icon name="check" class="mt-1 h-5 w-5 text-good-ink" />
          <span>Delete the stack and the access is gone. Nothing in Cloudoptics can recreate it.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="permissions" aria-labelledby="permissions-title" class={sectionCls}>
      <h2 id="permissions-title" class={h2}>Every permission</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-secondary">
        The full list from <code>cloudoptics-access.yaml</code> version {facts.templateVersion}, grouped by what it is for.
        The same data is published as <a class="text-link underline" href="/permissions.json">permissions.json</a>.
      </p>
      <PermissionTable role={billingRole} detail="full" />
      <PermissionTable role={resourceRole} detail="full" />

      <h3 class="mt-10 text-lg font-semibold">What Cloudoptics cannot do</h3>
      <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2">
        {permissions.cannot.map((c) => <li class="flex gap-2"><Icon name="x" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 text-critical-ink" />{c}</li>)}
      </ul>

      <div class="mt-10 rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-2 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">The setup step's role</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-secondary">
          The stack also creates a role for its setup function ({facts.setupRuntime?.replace('python', 'Python ')}). It
          runs only while CloudFormation creates, updates or deletes the stack, and Cloudoptics cannot assume it. Unlike
          the read roles it has write actions, because it creates the bill-export bucket, rolls the resource role out to
          member accounts and removes older Cloudoptics stacks. Where AWS allows, each action is limited to resources
          with Cloudoptics names; the "Applies to" column shows where it is not.
        </p>
        <PermissionTable role={setupRole} detail="full" headingLevel="h4" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="data" aria-labelledby="data-title" class={sectionCls}>
      <h2 id="data-title" class={h2}>Where data is kept</h2>
      <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-1 p-4">
          <dt class="font-semibold">Your bills</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-secondary">
            AWS writes the bill export to <code>{facts.bucketName}</code> in your own account. The bucket blocks public
            access, encrypts files, accepts HTTPS only, and its files expire after {facts.exportExpiryDays} days.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-1 p-4">
          <dt class="font-semibold">Cloudoptics</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-secondary">
            Cloudoptics runs on {claims.hosting}. Your browser and the stack's setup step talk to it over HTTPS.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-1 p-4">
          <dt class="font-semibold">What we read</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-secondary">
            Bill files, resource settings and tags, and usage metrics. Not the contents of your files, databases, secrets
            or logs.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-1 p-4">
          <dt class="font-semibold">Retention and sub-processors</dt>
          <dd class="mt-1 text-secondary">
            How long we keep data after you delete a workspace, and the companies that process data for us, will be set
            out in our privacy notice, which is being prepared.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">
        This public site sets no cookies and loads nothing from other websites. It counts page visits itself
        (first-party, no cookies): the page, the site you came from and any campaign tag, never your IP address or an
        identifier. It sends nothing if your browser asks not to be tracked.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="sign-in" aria-labelledby="sign-in-title" class={sectionCls}>
      <h2 id="sign-in-title" class={h2}>Sign-in and MFA</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-secondary">
        Sign-in uses WorkOS AuthKit, so Cloudoptics never stores your password. A workspace admin can require
        multi-factor authentication for everyone in the workspace; Cloudoptics then refuses requests from sessions
        without it. People in a workspace have admin, member or viewer roles, and changes such as connecting AWS need an
        admin.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="our-practice" aria-labelledby="our-practice-title" class={sectionCls}>
      <h2 id="our-practice-title" class={h2}>How we build Cloudoptics</h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 grid gap-3 md:grid-cols-2">
        <li class="rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-1 p-4">
          <strong>Workspace isolation in the database.</strong>
          <span class="text-secondary">PostgreSQL row-level security keeps each workspace's data apart, on every query.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-1 p-4">
          <strong>Audit log.</strong>
          <span class="text-secondary">Changes to members, connections and settings are recorded in an append-only, hash-chained log you can read under Settings.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-1 p-4">
          <strong>Checks on every change.</strong>
          <span class="text-secondary">Secret scanning, static analysis, dependency and infrastructure scanning run on every pull request.</span>
        </li>
        <li class="rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-1 p-4">
          <strong>Pinned dependencies.</strong>
          <span class="text-secondary">Lockfiles are frozen, GitHub Actions are pinned to commit hashes, and automated updates keep both current.</span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="reporting" aria-labelledby="reporting-title" class={sectionCls}>
      <h2 id="reporting-title" class={h2}>Reporting a vulnerability</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-secondary">
        We will publish a reporting address and a <code>security.txt</code> file before the beta opens to the public. The
        same contact will handle requests for our security pack.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="template" aria-labelledby="template-title" class={sectionCls}>
      <h2 id="template-title" class={h2}>Download the template</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-secondary">
        Read the exact file you will deploy before you deploy it. It is the same template the one-click link opens.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4">
        <a href={TEMPLATE_URL} class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center gap-2 rounded-sm border border-border-strong bg-surface-1 px-5 font-semibold text-primary hover:border-accent">
          cloudoptics-access.yaml, version {facts.templateVersion}
        </a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <div class={sectionCls}>
    <Faq items={SECURITY_FAQ} title="Security questions" />
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="cta" class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-5xl px-4 sm:px-6">
    <div class="rounded-md bg-accent-soft p-8 text-center">
      <h2 id="cta" class={h2}>Seen enough?</h2>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-3">
        <a href={START_URL} class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center rounded-sm bg-accent px-5 font-semibold text-on-accent hover:bg-accent-hover">
          Start free
        </a>
        <a href="/how-it-works" class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center rounded-sm border border-border-strong bg-surface-1 px-5 font-semibold text-primary hover:border-accent">
          Read how it works
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
